From 25c71cf4ad8424ae54ed12304b9756ae5db1e3a8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JustFoura <JustFoura.dev@proton.me>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 03:26:43 +1300
Subject: [PATCH 16/18] Prompt-path copy staging pool with batched submit
 (parity PASS, no win yet)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

LLAMA_VK_COPY_POOL=<n>: give the scheduler's host->device expert-weight copies
n staging slots, each batched behind one fence, and stop submitting them through
the per-copy ggml_vk_synchronize() that pins the stream to one copy deep.
Default 0 leaves upstream's path untouched.

Parity: byte-identical greedy output on every arm of all three benchmark runs.
Correct, but not faster, and the reason is structural rather than a tuning miss.

Per-slot (one submit+fence per copy) blocked on 13712 of 13716 acquires — the CPU
never got ahead. ~13,700 submit+fence round trips per pass cost more in queue
latency than the GPU work they wait for. Batching is the answer, but:

avg_batch=1.1 at 16 slots, 1.2 at 32. The correctness flush in
ggml_vk_get_compute_ctx fires between nearly every copy, so a batch never exceeds
one member — get_compute_ctx runs ~13,000 times per pass. Copies and compute
contexts alternate about 1:1, so there is no bulk copy phase to batch or overlap
at any slot count. Prefetching copy N+1 where copy N is consumed is the route.

Two bugs found and fixed getting here, both worth keeping in mind:
  - Flushing a batch ends the open transfer command buffer. reserve() must run
    before a caller takes a vk_context, or the caller records into a command
    buffer that has already been ended (this hung both arms of run 2).
  - Compute waits on transfer_semaphore at the value captured inside
    ggml_vk_get_compute_ctx(). A batch still open at that moment is submitted
    after the capture, so that compute never waits for it. Flush at the top.

Keep the change: it is safe and it is the shape the next build needs.

